{"name":"Ice Palace","hex":"#e2e4d7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ice palace","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A temporary structure, similar to a small palace or castle, made from blocks of ice."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ice_palace"}],"entries":[{"title":"The Ice House (St. Petersburg)","blocks":[{"text":"The Ice House was a palace built of ice in the winter of 1739–40 in Saint Petersburg, Russia. The palace and the surrounding festivities were part of the celebration of Russia's victory over the Ottoman Empire. The Empress Anna Ivanovna ordered Alexis Tatishchev, a court functionary, to construct it on the river Neva between the Admiralty and the Winter Palace during the bitterly cold winter of 1739–40. It was 80 feet long, 33 feet high, and 23 feet deep, and cost 30,000 rubles. By June 1740, the palace had melted into mere blocks of ice floating in the river."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Ledjanoj_dom.jpg/330px-Ledjanoj_dom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Ice_House_(St._Petersburg)","wikidata":"Q1313745"},{"title":"Ice Palace (Saint Petersburg)","blocks":[{"text":"The Ice Palace is an arena in Saint Petersburg, Russia. It was built for the 2000 IIHF World Championship and opened in 2000. It holds 12,300 people."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Ice_Palace_%28Saint_Petersburg%29_%28cropped%29.png/330px-Ice_Palace_%28Saint_Petersburg%29_%28cropped%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice_Palace_(Saint_Petersburg)","wikidata":"Q1320509"},{"title":"Ice palace","blocks":[{"text":"An ice palace or ice castle is a castle-like structure made of blocks of ice. These blocks are usually harvested from nearby rivers or lakes when they become frozen in winter. The first known ice palace appeared in St. Petersburg, Russia, when Anna Ivanovna, Empress of Russia, commissioned an ice palace in St. Petersburg, Russia, during the winter of 1739–40. Architect Piotr Eropkin and scientist Georg Wolfgang Krafft used huge ice blocks measuring 16 m (52 ft) long by 5 m (16 ft) wide, joined together with frozen water, to build the palace. The city recreates the ice palace every winter."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice_palace","wikidata":"Q28057462"},{"title":"Ice","blocks":[{"text":"Ice is water that is frozen into a solid state, typically forming at or below temperatures of 0 °C, 32 °F, or 273.15 K. It occurs naturally on Earth, on other planets, in Oort cloud objects, and as interstellar ice. As a naturally occurring crystalline inorganic solid with an ordered structure, ice is considered to be a mineral. Depending on the presence of impurities such as particles of soil or bubbles of air, it can appear transparent or a more or less opaque bluish-white color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg/330px-Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice","wikidata":"Q23392"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Valery_Jacobi_Ice_House.jpg/500px-Valery_Jacobi_Ice_House.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Valery_Jacobi_Ice_House.jpg/1280px-Valery_Jacobi_Ice_House.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Valery_Jacobi_Ice_House.jpg/500px-Valery_Jacobi_Ice_House.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Wedding of the Court Jester in the Ice House (Wedding at the House of Ice ; Valery Jacobi, 1878)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AValery_Jacobi_Ice_House.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Der_eispallast_in_st._petersburg.jpg/500px-Der_eispallast_in_st._petersburg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Der_eispallast_in_st._petersburg.jpg/1280px-Der_eispallast_in_st._petersburg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Der_eispallast_in_st._petersburg.jpg/500px-Der_eispallast_in_st._petersburg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Der Eispallast in St. Petersburg - Figure illustrating a report on the Ice House in as German illustrated popular magazine (1740)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADer_eispallast_in_st._petersburg.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/CWA%3B_%22The_New_Deal_Ice_Palace%22%3B_Lake_Bemidji%2C_Minnesota_-_DPLA_-_0746a82807f00fbe039585a2701afeaf.gif/500px-CWA%3B_%22The_New_Deal_Ice_Palace%22%3B_Lake_Bemidji%2C_Minnesota_-_DPLA_-_0746a82807f00fbe039585a2701afeaf.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/CWA%3B_%22The_New_Deal_Ice_Palace%22%3B_Lake_Bemidji%2C_Minnesota_-_DPLA_-_0746a82807f00fbe039585a2701afeaf.gif/500px-CWA%3B_%22The_New_Deal_Ice_Palace%22%3B_Lake_Bemidji%2C_Minnesota_-_DPLA_-_0746a82807f00fbe039585a2701afeaf.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Civil Works Administration: \"The Indian New Deal Ice Palace\", Lake Bemidji, Minnesota--1934","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACWA%3B_%22The_New_Deal_Ice_Palace%22%3B_Lake_Bemidji%2C_Minnesota_-_DPLA_-_0746a82807f00fbe039585a2701afeaf.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikime